readings.c1s1:
¶

{{smaller|'''To tone up''', to give a higher tone or character to; make more vigorous or forcible; heighten; strengthen.}}

II. ''intrans.'' 1. To take on a particular tone; specifically, to assume color or tint.

{{smaller block|If the prints are fumed in a box, and are left in too long, they will ''tone'' to a cold blue.{{right|''Lea'', Photography, p. 277.}}}}

2. To harmonize in tone, color, or tint.

{{smaller block|Beaded passementerie, which ''tones'' in with the delicate shades of blue, and pink chiffon, and dark velvet.{{right|''The Spectator'' (St. Louis), XI. 327.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To tone up''', to gain in tone, strength, or vigor.}}

{{smaller block|The Bensons passed through Washington the other day from the South, and spoke of going to Atlantic City to ''tone up'' a little before the season.{{right|''C. D. Warner'', Their Pilgrimage, p. 28.}}}}

'''tone<sup>2</sup>†''' (tōn), ''indef. pron.'' [ME. ''tone, ton, toon, tane'', in ''the tone'' (Sc. ''the tane''), a misdivision of ''thet one'', that one. Cf. ''tother''.] One: originally and usually preceded by ''the'', and usually followed by ''the tother''. See etymology. Compare ''tother''.

{{smaller block|Thou sulde doo bathe [both] . . . ''the tane'' and ''the tother''.{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 29.}}}}

{{smaller block|''The toon'' yeveth conysaunce,<br>And ''the tother'' ignoraunce.{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 5559.}}}}

{{smaller block|Many other thinges, touchyng the pestilent secte of Luther and Tyndale, by ''the tone'' bygone in Saxony: and by ''the tother'' laboured to be brought into England.{{right|''Sir T. More'', Worship of Images, Utopia, Int., p. xci.}}}}

'''tone-color''' (tōn′kul″o̤r), ''n.'' In ''musical acoustics'', same as ''timbre''.

{{smaller block|The variety of ''tone-colour'' . . . and the brilliant effects obtainable by a full-sized band of artist-performers.{{right|''Grove'', Dict. Music, IV. 472.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''toned''' (tōnd), ''a.'' [< ''tone''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having tone or a tone: much used in composition: as, high-''toned''; shrill-''toned''. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In a state of proper tension; strung.}}

{{smaller block|It may be doubted whether there ever existed a human being whose mind was quite as firmly ''toned'' at eighty as at forty.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xiv.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') Tinted; slightly colored: noting paper and other fabrics: as, a two-''toned'' ribbon. (''c'') In ''photog.'', treated with chemicals to improve the color.—'''Toned paper''', paper of a very pale amber tint, intermediate between warm buff and ivory-white.}}

{{smaller block|What is often called ''toned paper'' is nearer the natural color—a yellowish shade—of the pulp.
{{right|''Harper's Mag.'', LXXV. 120.}}}}

'''toneless''' (tōn′les), ''a.'' [< ''tone''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less''.] Without tone; unmodulated; unaccentuated.

{{smaller block|His voice . . . was to Grandcourt's ''toneless'' drawl . . . as the deep notes of a violoncello to the broken discourse of poultry and other lazy gentry in the afternoon sunshine.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Daniel Deronda, xxix.}}}}

'''tonelessness''' (tōn′les-nes), ''n.'' The quality or state of being toneless; lack of tone, in any sense.

{{smaller block|Any dulness or ''tonelessness'' on percussion at one apex must, in a doubtful case, be regarded as of great significance.
{{right|''Lancet'', 1889, II. 1294.}}}}

'''tone-master''' (tōn′mȧs″tėr), ''n.'' A master or expert in the artistic use of tones; a trained and experienced musical composer.

'''tone-measurer''' (tōn′mezh″ṳ̄r-ėr), ''n.'' Same as ''monochord''.

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|Thu havest clivers [claws] suthe stronge,
Thu tuengst [twingest] thar-mid so [as] doth a ''tonge.''
{{right|''Owl and Nightingale'' (ed. Wright), l. 156.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''tonges'' that drow the nayles out
Of fet, of handes, al about.
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 188.}}}}

{{smaller block|With that the wicked carle, the maister Smith,
A paire of red-whot yron ''tongs'' did take
Out of the burning cinders, and therewith
Under his side him nipt. {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. v. 44.}}}}

{{smaller block|He sat by the fireside, . . . writing the name of his mistress in the ashes with an old ''tongs'' that had lost one of its legs. {{right|''Irving'', Salmagundi, No. 2. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|Sure the shovel and ''tongs''
To each other belongs.
{{right|''Lover'', Widow Machree.}}}}

{{smaller block|[Tongs were formerly used in rough burlesque music:}}

{{smaller block|I have a reasonable good ear in music. Let 's have the ''tongs'' and the bones. {{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iv. 1. 32.]}}}}

2. In ''diamond-cutting'', a two-footed wooden stand that has at one end a vise-like iron holder, into which the dop containing the diamond is fastened, holding the diamond against the wheel.—3. ''pl.'' A device for anchoring the body of a car to the track when it is not in use. ''Car-Builder's Dict.''—4. ''pl.'' Trousers. [Slang, New Eng.]

{{smaller block|The boys dressed in ''tongs'', a name for pantaloons or overalls that had come into use. {{right|''S. Judd'', Margaret, i. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Asparagus-tongs''', a pair of tongs with broad flat blades, one of which has a hooked or turned-up end, to retain the stalks of asparagus. A spoon and a fork are sometimes hinged together in place of the blades.—'''Clam-tongs''', an instrument for tonging clams, like oyster-tongs, but differing in the width of the head, which averages 3½ feet.—'''Coral-tongs''', tongs used in the coral-fishery.—'''Dog-tongs.''' See the quotation.}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|We have never heard of ''dog tongs'' out of Wales. Mr. Owen figures one of these instruments, which it is not easy to describe without an illustration. They were used for catching dogs which were so ill-trained as to fight during the time of service.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., I. 479.}}}}

'''Hammer and tongs.''' {{smaller|See ''hammer''<sup>1</sup>.—}}'''Sardine-tongs,''' {{smaller|small tongs, like sugar-tongs but with broad flat blades, used for lifting sardines out of the box without breaking them.—}}'''Sliding tongs.''' {{smaller|See ''slide''.—}}'''Tourmalin tongs.''' {{smaller|See ''polariscope''.}}

'''tong<sup>1</sup>''' (tông), ''v.'' [< ''tong''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To seize, hold, or take with tongs.

{{smaller block|Though there is a planting interest at Mobile, Ala., most of the oysters on sale are of native growth, and ''tonged'' in a part of the bay called the "gully."
{{right|''Fisheries of U. S.'', V. ii. 548.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To handle or use tongs; capture something, as oysters, with tongs.

{{smaller block|He fishes, he ''tongs'' for oysters.
{{right|''Scribner's Mag.'', VIII. 512.}}}}

'''tong<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' An old spelling of ''tongue''.

'''tonga''' (tong′gä̤), ''n.'' [< Hind. ''tāngā''.] A light two-wheeled vehicle with wooden axletrees, drawn by ponies or oxen, and much used on the up-country roads in British India.

{{smaller block|The Himalayan ''tonga'' is a thing of delight. It is easily described, for in principle it is the ancient Persian war-chariot, though the accommodation is so modified as to allow four persons to sit in it back to back.
{{right|''F. M. Crawford'', Mr. Isaacs, ix.}}}}

'''Tonga bean''' (tong′gä̤ bēn). See ''tonka-bean''.

